    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d941">

                <pb facs="0839" n="827" xml:id="pg827"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/20395: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi><anchor n="47" xml:id="d941-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-03-17T12:00:00+03:00">March 17,
                        1942—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 12:48 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>232. I informed Vyshinski<anchor n="48" xml:id="d941-f2"/> last night of the
                    report regarding the recent attempt on Von Papen’s life as presented in the
                    Department’s 122, March 14.<anchor n="49" xml:id="d941-f3"/> Vyshinski expressed
                    great appreciation, and said the information confirmed other reports reaching
                    the Soviet Government indicative of efforts by Germany to provoke a conflict
                    between Russia and Turkey.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d941-f1" n="47" type="source" xml:id="d941-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to the Ambassador in Turkey as Department’s telegram No. 85, March 19, 4
                    p.m.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d941-f2" n="48" xml:id="d941-f2-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs
                    of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d941-f3" n="49" xml:id="d941-f3-content">
                <p>See footnote 43, <ref target="#pg825">p. 825</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
